//! block — the block layer: the devices, a request and its completion,
//! the partition table (px66) and the buffer cache (px63).
//!
//! **The devices.** A table of DEVMAX (129) records, one per disk a
//! driver brought up and one per partition its table names: the disk's
//! driver (kernel/vblk, the only one), its capacity in 512-byte sectors
//! (the unit every request is in, whatever the disk's own block size),
//! whether it is read-only and whether it takes a FLUSH, its name (`vda`,
//! `vdb`, …: Linux's names for virtio disks, so an `/etc/fstab` line
//! reads as it would there; a partition's is its disk's and its number,
//! `vda2`) and Linux's device numbers for it (254:0, 254:16, … for a
//! disk, 254:N for its partition N up to 15 and the extended major 259
//! from 0 up for the rest, in order: measured on the Linux guest, where
//! virtio_blk's dynamic major is 254 and each disk takes 16 minors).
//! `start` brings up what is there; a machine with no disk has none, and
//! nothing is printed.
//!
//! **A partition is a window** (px66): its first sector on its disk and
//! its sectors. Every request to it is bounds-checked against its own
//! sectors before it is made and then offset by its start, so nothing
//! outside the window can be read or written through it; a request past
//! its end is -EIO, counted, one line once a device, as a request past a
//! disk's capacity is. The disk stays a device of its own (the whole of
//! it), and the two never mount at once: `claim` refuses a disk while one
//! of its partitions is mounted and a partition while its disk is, by
//! name (-EBUSY), because their blocks would be cached twice.
//!
//! **The partition table** (px66), read once at `start` from the disk's
//! first sectors, written from the UEFI Specification 2.10 §5:
//!
//! - LBA 0, the MBR: 0x55aa at byte 510 or there is no table (a bare
//!   file system, a blank disk: nothing is said). Its four records
//!   (§5.2.1) are each 16 bytes from byte 446; a record whose boot flag
//!   is neither 0 nor 0x80 means the sector is not a partition table (a
//!   FAT boot sector, say), and nothing is read. One record of OS type
//!   0xEE starting at LBA 1 is the protective MBR (§5.2.3): the disk is
//!   GPT. Otherwise it is an MBR-only disk (R-px66-1, recommended and
//!   implemented, not decided: read as the Linux guest reads it, its
//!   primary records only): each record with a type and a size is
//!   partition 1-4, its PARTUUID the disk signature (byte 440) and its
//!   number (`5ca1ab1e-01`, as the guest's blkid prints it); an extended
//!   record (0x05, 0x0f, 0x85) is refused by name, its logical
//!   partitions not read.
//! - The primary header at LBA 1 and the backup at the last LBA
//!   (§5.3.2), each checked in the specification's order, every check a
//!   named refusal: the signature `EFI PART`; a revision of major 1; the
//!   header size, 92 to 512; the header's CRC32 over that size with its
//!   own field zero; MyLBA the LBA it was read from; the usable range
//!   inside the disk; an entry size of 128 or a larger multiple of 8 (as
//!   the specification allowed before 2.10 asked for 128 times a power of
//!   two; at most 4096 here); the entry array (count × size, at most 1 MiB
//!   here) inside the disk and outside the usable range; the array's
//!   CRC32. The primary is used when it holds, and a backup that does not
//!   is said once; when the primary fails and the backup holds, the
//!   backup is used and said so once (§5.3.2: "software must check the
//!   last LBA"; measured: Linux 7.2.8 reads the backup only when booted
//!   with `gpt`, and never reads entries larger than 128 bytes); when
//!   both fail there are no partitions, said once, and the disk is still
//!   a device, the whole of it.
//! - The entries (§5.3.3): an entry whose type GUID is zero is unused;
//!   any other is partition N (N its index plus one, gaps and all, as
//!   Linux names them), unless its first LBA is after its last or it
//!   lies outside the usable range (refused by name, counted). Each keeps
//!   its type GUID, its unique GUID (the PARTUUID, printed as Appendix A
//!   writes a GUID: the first three fields little-endian, lower case),
//!   its first LBA and sectors, its attributes and its name (UTF-16LE,
//!   36 units, kept as UTF-8: the PARTLABEL). Overlaps are not checked
//!   (named: the specification forbids them, and nothing here relies on
//!   their absence).
//! - A disk whose logical block is not 512 bytes (VIRTIO_BLK_F_BLK_SIZE)
//!   is refused by name: its table is not read and it stays whole
//!   (measured: Linux on the same image and device finds no partitions
//!   either, since its table is at LBA 1 of 512 bytes).
//!
//! **A request** is a record of 8 words (kernel/vblk reads and writes
//! them): its type (IN, OUT, FLUSH), its first sector, its sectors, the
//! data's physical address (inside one frame), the status the device
//! wrote, and its state: free, with the device, done. `rw` and `flush`
//! fill one, hand it to the driver and wait for its completion. The
//! completion is the record's state word, written by the driver when it
//! takes the request back from the used ring; the wait looks for it
//! (polled: kernel/vblk says why) and a request that never comes back is
//! an I/O error by name, never a hang. A request past the device's
//! capacity, or a write to a read-only device, is refused before it is
//! made (-EIO, counted, one line once). A partition's requests are
//! counted on it and on its disk (the disk's books are every request its
//! driver was given).
//!
//! **The buffer cache.** A file system reads and writes its blocks (1,
//! 2 or 4 KiB) through `get`, `fresh`, `dirty` and `sync`: up to NB
//! (512) buffers, a frame each, taken as they are first needed and
//! never given back; a buffer is (device, block size, block number),
//! found through a hash of 512 chains. A miss takes a free buffer or,
//! when all are made, the clock hand's next one not looked at since it
//! last passed (second chance), writing it first when it is dirty.
//! **A buffer's address is good until the next `get` or `fresh`**: the
//! callers (kernel/ext2) copy out what they need, or write and mark the
//! buffer dirty, before asking for another; nothing is pinned.
//!
//! **Writing (R-px63-3).** The policy is a word: write-through (the
//! default) writes a buffer the moment it is marked dirty, so the disk
//! holds every change a system call made before the call returns;
//! write-back marks it and writes it at `sync` (fsync, syncfs, sync,
//! unmount, halt, reboot) or when the clock hand takes it. `sync` writes
//! every dirty buffer of a device, in the order of its slots, then sends the
//! device a FLUSH when it takes one, so what is written is on the disk's
//! medium, not in its cache. The counters say what each policy cost.
//!
//! **The journal's buffers (px68).** kernel/journal marks a metadata
//! block it holds for the running transaction (`jdirty`: B_JRUN, and
//! B_JMETA with it): such a buffer is never written to its home, by
//! `sync`, by write-through or by the clock hand, and never evicted,
//! until the journal has logged it and its commit block is on the disk
//! (`jdone`); it is then a committed block awaiting its checkpoint
//! (B_JMETA alone), which the clock hand and `checkpoint` may write
//! home. `write_data` writes the dirty buffers that are neither (a
//! journalled volume's file data: data=ordered, written before the
//! commit that names it). `jforget` takes a block freed in the running
//! transaction back out of it, its changes dropped (the block is free;
//! what the disk holds is what the last commit left).
//!
//! Every entry runs with IF clear on one CPU (a system call, or a
//! kernel's start). No region, no heap: the state is frames this module
//! takes at `start`, their addresses in module `var`s (scalars).
//!
//! Written from the OASIS VIRTIO specification 1.2 §5.2.6 (the request's
//! unit and FLUSH, through kernel/vblk), the UEFI Specification 2.10 §5
//! (the MBR, the protective MBR, the GPT header and entry array) and
//! Appendix A (a GUID's text), `fsync(2)`, `sync(2)`, `syncfs(2)`,
//! `proc(5)` (/proc/partitions) and black-box runs of Linux 7.2.8 on
//! QEMU (the device numbers, the names, /proc/partitions' layout, what a
//! damaged table reads as: `notes/px63/`, `notes/px66/`,
//! tests/mpx6-part.d/linux/). No Linux source (none of its block layer or
//! partition code); no other kernel's source; no partitioner's source.

use frames
use log
use vblk

extern "c" fn pax_tree_copy(dst: u64, src: u64, n: u64)
extern "c" fn pax_tree_zero(dst: u64, n: u64)

const PAGE: u64 = 4096
/// The most devices: one disk and its partitions.
pub const DEVMAX: u64 = 129
/// No device (by_name's and the lookups' answer when none matches).
pub const DEVS: u64 = 129
/// The most buffers.
pub const NB: u64 = 512
const HPF: u64 = 128 // buffer headers a frame (4 words each)
const BUCKETS: u64 = 512

/// errno (uapi asm-generic/errno-base.h).
const EIO: u64 = 5
const EBUSY: u64 = 16
const ENOSPC: u64 = 28

// The state frame's words.
const S_DEVS: u64 = 0 // devices in the table
const S_POLICY: u64 = 1 // 0 write-through, 1 write-back
const S_MADE: u64 = 2 // buffers made
const S_HAND: u64 = 3 // the clock hand
const S_HASH: u64 = 4 // the hash frame (physical): BUCKETS heads, a slot + 1 each
const S_HDRS: u64 = 5 // the header frames (physical), NB / HPF of them, from here
const S_COUNT: u64 = 16 // the cache's counters (K_*), from here
const S_DEVF: u64 = 24 // the device table's frames (physical), DEVF of them, from here
const DEVF: u64 = 17
const DPF: u64 = 8 // device records a frame
const S_SCR: u64 = 41 // the table's scratch frame (physical)
// The partition table read (px66).
const S_PT_KIND: u64 = 42 // PT_*
const S_PT_USED: u64 = 43 // the header used: 1 primary, 2 backup
const S_PT_WHYP: u64 = 44 // why the primary was refused (W_*), 0 it holds
const S_PT_WHYB: u64 = 45 // the backup's
const S_PT_VALP: u64 = 46 // a number the primary's refusal names (found), and 47 (wanted)
const S_PT_VALB: u64 = 48 // the backup's, and 49
const S_PT_BADN: u64 = 50 // entries refused
const S_PT_BADK: u64 = 51 // the first refused entry's slot (1-based)
const S_PT_BADW: u64 = 52 // and why (E_*)
const S_PT_OVER: u64 = 53 // used entries past DEVMAX
const S_PT_ALT: u64 = 54 // the primary's AlternateLBA + 1 when it is not the last LBA
const S_PT_DIFF: u64 = 55 // both hold and disagree
const S_PT_EXT: u64 = 56 // an extended MBR record refused: slot | type << 8
const S_PT_SIG: u64 = 57 // the MBR's disk signature
const S_PT_EXTN: u64 = 58 // the next extended minor (major 259)
const S_WB: u64 = 59 // the entry array reader's window: first sector, sectors
const S_WN: u64 = 60
const S_AELBA: u64 = 61 // the array being read: its LBA, its last sector + 1
const S_AEND: u64 = 62
const S_AERR: u64 = 63 // a read of it failed
// A header's facts (H_P the primary's, H_B the backup's), HX_* words each.
const H_P: u64 = 64
const H_B: u64 = 80
const HX_MY: u64 = 0
const HX_ALT: u64 = 1
const HX_FIRST: u64 = 2
const HX_LAST: u64 = 3
const HX_GUID: u64 = 4 // two words: the disk GUID's 16 bytes as two le64
const HX_ELBA: u64 = 6
const HX_COUNT: u64 = 7
const HX_ESIZE: u64 = 8
const HX_ACRC: u64 = 9
const HX_HCRC: u64 = 10
const S_PINNED: u64 = 104 // px68: misses that found every buffer held by a running transaction
const S_REQ: u64 = 128 // the request record (8 words, kernel/vblk's layout)

// What the table read found.
const PT_NONE: u64 = 0 // no 0x55aa: no table (silent)
const PT_GPT: u64 = 1
const PT_MBR: u64 = 2
const PT_GPTBAD: u64 = 3 // a protective MBR and neither header holds
const PT_LBS: u64 = 4 // logical blocks not 512 bytes: not read
const PT_NOTMBR: u64 = 5 // 0x55aa but a boot flag neither 0 nor 0x80 (silent)
const PT_IO: u64 = 6 // LBA 0 could not be read

// Why a header was refused.
const W_IO: u64 = 1
const W_SIG: u64 = 2
const W_REV: u64 = 3
const W_HSIZE: u64 = 4
const W_HCRC: u64 = 5
const W_MYLBA: u64 = 6
const W_USABLE: u64 = 7
const W_ESIZE: u64 = 8
const W_ASIZE: u64 = 9
const W_ARRAY: u64 = 10
const W_ACRC: u64 = 11

// Why an entry was refused.
const E_ORDER: u64 = 1 // its first LBA after its last
const E_USABLE: u64 = 2 // outside the usable range
const E_DISK: u64 = 3 // (MBR) past the disk's end, or at LBA 0

// A device record's words.
const D_PRESENT: u64 = 0
const D_SECTORS: u64 = 1
const D_FLAGS: u64 = 2 // F_RO, F_FLUSH
const D_LBS: u64 = 3 // the device's logical block size
const D_READS: u64 = 4 // requests read, written, flushed, failed
const D_WRITES: u64 = 5
const D_FLUSHES: u64 = 6
const D_ERRORS: u64 = 7
const D_SIN: u64 = 8 // sectors read, written
const D_SOUT: u64 = 9
const D_REFUSED: u64 = 10 // requests refused before they were made
const D_DISK: u64 = 11 // the disk's device (itself for a disk)
const D_START: u64 = 12 // the first sector on the disk (0 for a disk)
const D_PARTNO: u64 = 13 // the partition's number (0 for a disk)
const D_MAJOR: u64 = 14
const D_MINOR: u64 = 15
const D_TYPE: u64 = 16 // GPT: the type GUID (two le64); MBR: the OS type
const D_UUID: u64 = 18 // GPT: the unique GUID (two le64); MBR: the signature, the number
const D_ATTR: u64 = 20
const D_KIND: u64 = 21 // K_DISK, K_GPT, K_MBR
const D_CLAIM: u64 = 22 // mounted (claim)
const D_SAID: u64 = 23 // its refusals said (one line each kind)
const D_NAMELEN: u64 = 24 // the partition's name (GPT), UTF-8, from D_NAME
const D_NAME: u64 = 25
const NAME_MAX: u64 = 312 // bytes: the 39 words from D_NAME
const DW: u64 = 64
/// Device kinds.
pub const KIND_DISK: u64 = 0
pub const KIND_GPT: u64 = 1
pub const KIND_MBR: u64 = 2
/// Device flags.
pub const F_RO: u64 = 1
pub const F_FLUSH: u64 = 2

// A buffer header's words.
const H_KEY: u64 = 0 // (dev + 1) << 56 | shift << 48 | block; 0 none
const H_PA: u64 = 1 // its frame
const H_FLAGS: u64 = 2
const H_NEXT: u64 = 3 // the hash chain: a slot + 1, 0 the end
const B_VALID: u64 = 1
const B_DIRTY: u64 = 2
const B_REF: u64 = 4
const B_JRUN: u64 = 8 // px68: in the running transaction: never written home or evicted
const B_JMETA: u64 = 16 // px68: a journalled volume's metadata (logged, awaiting its checkpoint)

/// The cache's counters: buffers found, read from the disk, taken by
/// the hand, written (marked dirty, then written), marked dirty while
/// dirty already (write-back's saving), FLUSH requests.
pub const K_HITS: u64 = 0
pub const K_MISSES: u64 = 1
pub const K_EVICTS: u64 = 2
pub const K_WRITES: u64 = 3
pub const K_REDIRTY: u64 = 4
pub const K_FLUSHES: u64 = 5
pub const K_DIRTY: u64 = 6 // dirty now
pub const K_FRESH: u64 = 7 // buffers made without a read (a new block)

/// Policies (R-px63-3).
pub const WRITE_THROUGH: u64 = 0
pub const WRITE_BACK: u64 = 1

var state: u64 = 0

fn base() -> u64 {
    // # Safety: a scalar module var, read on one CPU with IF clear.
    unsafe { state }
}

fn st(i: u64) -> u64 {
    // # Safety: i < 512, a word of the state frame this module owns.
    unsafe { ((base() + i * 8) as *u64).read_volatile() }
}

fn put_st(i: u64, v: u64) {
    // # Safety: as `st`.
    unsafe { ((base() + i * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(v) }
}

fn rd(a: u64) -> u64 {
    // # Safety: a word of a frame this module owns, through the HHDM.
    unsafe { (a as *u64).read_volatile() }
}

fn wr(a: u64, v: u64) {
    // # Safety: as `rd`.
    unsafe { (a as *u64).write_volatile(v) }
}

fn hh() -> u64 {
    frames.hhdm()
}

fn rec(d: u64) -> u64 {
    st(S_DEVF + d / DPF) + hh() + (d % DPF) * DW * 8
}

fn dw(d: u64, w: u64) -> u64 {
    rd(rec(d) + w * 8)
}

fn put_dw(d: u64, w: u64, v: u64) {
    wr(rec(d) + w * 8, v)
}

fn bump_dw(d: u64, w: u64, by: u64) {
    put_dw(d, w, dw(d, w) + by)
}

fn kbump(i: u64, by: u64) {
    put_st(S_COUNT + i, st(S_COUNT + i) + by)
}

/// Cache counter `i` (K_*).
pub fn counter(i: u64) -> u64 {
    if base() == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    st(S_COUNT + i)
}

fn zeroed() -> u64 {
    let pa = frames.alloc() else 0
    if pa != 0 {
        // # Safety: the frame just taken, through the HHDM.
        unsafe { pax_tree_zero(pa + hh(), PAGE) }
    }
    pa
}

fn b8(a: u64) -> u64 {
    // # Safety: a byte of a frame this module owns (the scratch frame,
    // a device record), or of the caller's kernel buffer, through the HHDM.
    let v = unsafe { (a as *u8).read_volatile() }
    v as u64
}

fn put_b8(a: u64, v: u64) {
    // # Safety: as `b8`.
    unsafe { (a as *u8).write_volatile((v & 0xff) as u8) }
}

fn le16(a: u64) -> u64 {
    b8(a) | (b8(a + 1) << 8)
}

fn le32(a: u64) -> u64 {
    le16(a) | (le16(a + 2) << 16)
}

fn le64(a: u64) -> u64 {
    le32(a) | (le32(a + 4) << 32)
}

/// Bring up the disks on the paging structures at `pml4` (after
/// `pci.scan`, IF clear), and read each one's partition table: how many
/// disks there are. The state frames are taken only when there is one.
pub fn start(pml4: u64) -> u64 {
    if base() != 0 {
        return if st(S_DEVS) != 0 { 1 } else { 0 }
    }
    let r = vblk.start(pml4)
    if r != vblk.UP {
        return 0
    }
    let sf = zeroed()
    let hf = zeroed()
    if sf == 0 || hf == 0 {
        log.put("block: no frame for the block layer's state, frames free ")
        log.dec(frames.free_count() as int)
        log.end()
        return 0
    }
    // # Safety: a scalar module var, written once at boot with IF clear.
    unsafe { state = sf + hh() }
    put_st(S_HASH, hf)
    var k: u64 = 0
    while k < NB / HPF {
        let f = zeroed()
        if f == 0 {
            log.put("block: no frame for the buffer headers")
            log.end()
            return 0
        }
        put_st(S_HDRS + k, f)
        k += 1
    }
    k = 0
    while k <= DEVF {
        let f = zeroed()
        if f == 0 {
            log.put("block: no frame for the device table")
            log.end()
            return 0
        }
        if k == DEVF {
            put_st(S_SCR, f)
        } else {
            put_st(S_DEVF + k, f)
        }
        k += 1
    }
    put_dw(0, D_PRESENT, 1)
    put_dw(0, D_SECTORS, vblk.sectors())
    put_dw(0, D_LBS, vblk.block_size())
    put_dw(0, D_FLAGS, (if vblk.read_only() { F_RO } else { 0 }) | (if vblk.flushes() { F_FLUSH } else { 0 }))
    put_dw(0, D_DISK, 0)
    put_dw(0, D_MAJOR, 254)
    put_dw(0, D_MINOR, 0)
    put_dw(0, D_KIND, KIND_DISK)
    put_st(S_DEVS, 1)
    read_table()
    1
}

/// How many devices there are: the disks and their partitions.
pub fn count() -> u64 {
    if base() == 0 { 0 } else { st(S_DEVS) }
}

/// Device `d`'s capacity in 512-byte sectors (a partition's: its window).
pub fn sectors(d: u64) -> u64 {
    if d >= count() { 0 } else { dw(d, D_SECTORS) }
}

/// Its flags (F_RO, F_FLUSH: a partition's are its disk's).
pub fn flags(d: u64) -> u64 {
    if d >= count() { 0 } else { dw(d, D_FLAGS) }
}

/// Its kind: KIND_DISK, KIND_GPT or KIND_MBR (a partition of either table).
pub fn kind(d: u64) -> u64 {
    if d >= count() { KIND_DISK } else { dw(d, D_KIND) }
}

/// The disk device `d` is on (itself for a disk).
pub fn disk_of(d: u64) -> u64 {
    if d >= count() { d } else { dw(d, D_DISK) }
}

/// Its first sector on its disk (0 for a disk).
pub fn start_of(d: u64) -> u64 {
    if d >= count() { 0 } else { dw(d, D_START) }
}

/// Its partition number (0 for a disk).
pub fn partno(d: u64) -> u64 {
    if d >= count() { 0 } else { dw(d, D_PARTNO) }
}

fn digits(v: u64) -> u64 {
    var n: u64 = 1
    var x = v
    while x >= 10 {
        x = x / 10
        n += 1
    }
    n
}

/// The length of device `d`'s name (`vda`: 3, `vda12`: 5).
pub fn name_len(d: u64) -> u64 {
    let p = partno(d)
    if p == 0 { 3 } else { 3 + digits(p) }
}

/// Byte `i` of device `d`'s name (`vda`, `vda2`), 0 past the end.
pub fn name_byte(d: u64, i: u64) -> u64 {
    if i == 0 {
        return 118 // v
    }
    if i == 1 {
        return 100 // d
    }
    if i == 2 {
        return 97 + disk_of(d) // a, b, …
    }
    let p = partno(d)
    if p == 0 || i >= name_len(d) {
        return 0
    }
    // The number's digit i - 3 from the left.
    var x = p
    var k = name_len(d) - 1
    while k > i {
        x = x / 10
        k -= 1
    }
    48 + x % 10
}

/// Write device `d`'s name to the log.
pub fn put_name(d: u64) {
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < name_len(d) {
        log.put_byte(name_byte(d, i) as int)
        i += 1
    }
}

/// px67: device `d`'s name written at `base + at` (at most `cap` bytes
/// in all): the end (kernel/files' mount table).
pub fn name_into(d: u64, base: u64, at: u64, cap: u64) -> u64 {
    var k = at
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < name_len(d) {
        if k < cap {
            // # Safety: the caller's frame through the HHDM, `cap` bytes; k < cap.
            unsafe { ((base + k) as *u8).write_volatile((name_byte(d, i) & 0xff) as u8) }
        }
        k += 1
        i += 1
    }
    k
}

/// The device whose name is the `n` bytes at `a` (`vda`, `vda2`, or
/// `/dev/vda2`), or DEVS.
pub fn by_name(a: u64, n: u64) -> u64 {
    var o: u64 = 0
    if n > 5 && b8(a) == 47 && b8(a + 1) == 100 && b8(a + 2) == 101 && b8(a + 3) == 118 && b8(a + 4) == 47 {
        o = 5
    }
    if n < o + 3 || b8(a + o) != 118 || b8(a + o + 1) != 100 {
        return DEVS
    }
    let letter = b8(a + o + 2)
    if letter < 97 || letter > 122 {
        return DEVS
    }
    // The number, if any: digits, no leading zero.
    var p: u64 = 0
    var k = o + 3
    while k < n {
        let c = b8(a + k)
        if c < 48 || c > 57 || (k == o + 3 && c == 48) || p > 100000 {
            return DEVS
        }
        p = p * 10 + (c - 48)
        k += 1
    }
    var d: u64 = 0
    while d < count() {
        if disk_of(d) == letter - 97 && partno(d) == p {
            return d
        }
        d += 1
    }
    DEVS
}

/// Linux's device numbers for device `d`: (major, minor).
pub fn numbers(d: u64) -> (u64, u64) {
    if d >= count() {
        return (254, d * 16)
    }
    (dw(d, D_MAJOR), dw(d, D_MINOR))
}

fn said(d: u64, bit: u64) -> bool {
    let s = dw(d, D_SAID)
    put_dw(d, D_SAID, s | bit)
    s & bit != 0
}

/// One request to device `d`, waited for: type `op` (vblk.T_IN, T_OUT),
/// `n` sectors from `sector` (of the device: a partition's from its own
/// first sector), the data at physical `pa` (inside one frame: n <= 8).
/// 0, or -EIO (the device's error, a request that never came back, or a
/// refusal: past the capacity, a write to a read-only disk), each
/// counted.
pub fn rw(d: u64, op: u64, sector: u64, n: u64, pa: u64) -> u64 {
    if d >= count() {
        return neg(EIO)
    }
    let cap = dw(d, D_SECTORS)
    if n == 0 || n > 8 || n > cap || sector > cap - n {
        bump_dw(d, D_REFUSED, 1)
        if !said(d, 1) {
            log.put("block: refused: a request past the end of ")
            put_name(d)
            log.put(" (sector ")
            log.dec(sector as int)
            log.put(", ")
            log.dec(n as int)
            log.put(" sectors, capacity ")
            log.dec(cap as int)
            log.put("), -EIO")
            log.end()
        }
        return neg(EIO)
    }
    if op == vblk.T_OUT && dw(d, D_FLAGS) & F_RO != 0 {
        bump_dw(d, D_REFUSED, 1)
        if !said(d, 2) {
            log.put("block: refused: a write to ")
            put_name(d)
            log.put(", which the device says is read-only (VIRTIO_BLK_F_RO), -EIO")
            log.end()
        }
        return neg(EIO)
    }
    let disk = dw(d, D_DISK)
    let r = base() + S_REQ * 8
    wr(r, op)
    wr(r + 8, dw(d, D_START) + sector)
    wr(r + 16, n)
    wr(r + 24, pa)
    vblk.submit(r)
    let s = vblk.wait(r)
    wr(r + 40, vblk.RS_FREE)
    var x = d
    while true {
        if op == vblk.T_IN {
            bump_dw(x, D_READS, 1)
            bump_dw(x, D_SIN, n)
        } else {
            bump_dw(x, D_WRITES, 1)
            bump_dw(x, D_SOUT, n)
        }
        if s != vblk.S_OK {
            bump_dw(x, D_ERRORS, 1)
        }
        if x == disk {
            break
        }
        x = disk
    }
    if s != vblk.S_OK {
        return neg(EIO)
    }
    0
}

/// A FLUSH to device `d`'s disk (when it takes one: 0 at once when it
/// does not, its writes are then on the medium when they complete),
/// waited for: 0 or -EIO.
pub fn flush(d: u64) -> u64 {
    if d >= count() {
        return neg(EIO)
    }
    let disk = dw(d, D_DISK)
    if dw(disk, D_FLAGS) & F_FLUSH == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let r = base() + S_REQ * 8
    wr(r, vblk.T_FLUSH)
    wr(r + 8, 0)
    wr(r + 16, 0)
    wr(r + 24, 0)
    vblk.submit(r)
    let s = vblk.wait(r)
    wr(r + 40, vblk.RS_FREE)
    bump_dw(disk, D_FLUSHES, 1)
    kbump(K_FLUSHES, 1)
    if s != vblk.S_OK {
        bump_dw(disk, D_ERRORS, 1)
        return neg(EIO)
    }
    0
}

fn neg(e: u64) -> u64 {
    0xffffffffffffffff - e + 1
}

/// Device record word `w` of device `d` (a report's: D_READS and on).
pub fn books(d: u64, which: u64) -> u64 {
    if d >= count() {
        return 0
    }
    dw(d, D_READS + which)
}

// --- mounts: a disk or one of its partitions, never both -------------------------

/// Claim device `d` for a mount (kernel/ext2's): 0, or -EBUSY with one
/// line, by name, when `d` is a disk one of whose partitions is
/// mounted, or a partition whose disk is (their blocks would be cached
/// twice, under two keys). A second claim of `d` itself is kernel/ext2's
/// own -EBUSY, not said here.
pub fn claim(d: u64) -> u64 {
    if d >= count() {
        return neg(EIO)
    }
    let disk = dw(d, D_DISK)
    var x: u64 = 0
    while x < count() {
        if x != d && dw(x, D_DISK) == disk && dw(x, D_CLAIM) != 0 && (x == disk || d == disk) {
            log.put("block: refused: a mount of ")
            put_name(d)
            if d == disk {
                log.put(" while its partition ")
                put_name(x)
                log.put(" is mounted, -EBUSY")
            } else {
                log.put(" while ")
                put_name(x)
                log.put(", its disk, is mounted, -EBUSY")
            }
            log.end()
            return neg(EBUSY)
        }
        x += 1
    }
    put_dw(d, D_CLAIM, 1)
    0
}

/// Let device `d` go (an unmount).
pub fn release(d: u64) {
    if d < count() {
        put_dw(d, D_CLAIM, 0)
    }
}

// --- the partition table (px66) ----------------------------------------------------

fn crc_byte(c: u64, b: u64) -> u64 {
    var x = c ^ b
    var k: u64 = 0
    while k < 8 {
        if x & 1 != 0 {
            x = (x >> 1) ^ 0xedb88320
        } else {
            x = x >> 1
        }
        k += 1
    }
    x
}

fn scr() -> u64 {
    st(S_SCR) + hh()
}

/// Read `n` sectors of the disk from `lba` into the scratch frame: 0 or
/// -EIO. The array reader's window is forgotten.
fn read_scr(lba: u64, n: u64) -> u64 {
    put_st(S_WN, 0)
    rw(0, vblk.T_IN, lba, n, st(S_SCR))
}

/// Byte `o` of the entry array that starts at S_AELBA, through a window
/// of up to eight sectors in the scratch frame (S_AERR set on a failed read).
fn arr_byte(o: u64) -> u64 {
    let s = st(S_AELBA) + o / 512
    let wb = st(S_WB)
    if st(S_WN) == 0 || s < wb || s >= wb + st(S_WN) {
        var n = st(S_AEND) - s
        if n > 8 {
            n = 8
        }
        if rw(0, vblk.T_IN, s, n, st(S_SCR)) != 0 {
            put_st(S_AERR, 1)
            put_st(S_WN, 0)
            return 0
        }
        put_st(S_WB, s)
        put_st(S_WN, n)
    }
    b8(scr() + (s - st(S_WB)) * 512 + o % 512)
}

fn arr_le64(o: u64) -> u64 {
    var v: u64 = 0
    var k: u64 = 8
    while k > 0 {
        k -= 1
        v = (v << 8) | arr_byte(o + k)
    }
    v
}

fn arr_le32(o: u64) -> u64 {
    arr_byte(o) | (arr_byte(o + 1) << 8) | (arr_byte(o + 2) << 16) | (arr_byte(o + 3) << 24)
}

fn arr_open(elba: u64, bytes: u64) {
    put_st(S_AELBA, elba)
    put_st(S_AEND, elba + (bytes + 511) / 512)
    put_st(S_WN, 0)
    put_st(S_AERR, 0)
}

fn hx(h: u64, w: u64) -> u64 {
    st(h + w)
}

fn put_hx(h: u64, w: u64, v: u64) {
    put_st(h + w, v)
}

/// The header at `lba` checked (§5.3.2's tests, in its order) and its
/// facts kept at `h`: 0 when it holds, else why (W_*), with the numbers
/// the refusal names at `val` (found, wanted).
fn check_header(lba: u64, h: u64, val: u64) -> u64 {
    put_st(val, 0)
    put_st(val + 1, 0)
    let last = dw(0, D_SECTORS) - 1
    if read_scr(lba, 1) != 0 {
        return W_IO
    }
    let p = scr()
    if le64(p) != 0x5452415020494645 {
        put_st(val, le64(p))
        return W_SIG
    }
    let rev = le32(p + 8)
    if rev >> 16 != 1 {
        put_st(val, rev)
        return W_REV
    }
    let hs = le32(p + 12)
    if hs < 92 || hs > 512 {
        put_st(val, hs)
        return W_HSIZE
    }
    var c: u64 = 0xffffffff
    var k: u64 = 0
    while k < hs {
        c = crc_byte(c, if k >= 16 && k < 20 { 0 } else { b8(p + k) })
        k += 1
    }
    c = c ^ 0xffffffff
    let found = le32(p + 16)
    put_hx(h, HX_HCRC, found)
    if c != found {
        put_st(val, found)
        put_st(val + 1, c)
        return W_HCRC
    }
    let my = le64(p + 24)
    if my != lba {
        put_st(val, my)
        put_st(val + 1, lba)
        return W_MYLBA
    }
    put_hx(h, HX_MY, my)
    put_hx(h, HX_ALT, le64(p + 32))
    let first = le64(p + 40)
    let lastu = le64(p + 48)
    put_hx(h, HX_FIRST, first)
    put_hx(h, HX_LAST, lastu)
    put_hx(h, HX_GUID, le64(p + 56))
    put_hx(h, HX_GUID + 1, le64(p + 64))
    let elba = le64(p + 72)
    let count = le32(p + 80)
    let esize = le32(p + 84)
    let acrc = le32(p + 88)
    put_hx(h, HX_ELBA, elba)
    put_hx(h, HX_COUNT, count)
    put_hx(h, HX_ESIZE, esize)
    put_hx(h, HX_ACRC, acrc)
    if first < 2 || first > lastu || lastu >= last {
        put_st(val, first)
        put_st(val + 1, lastu)
        return W_USABLE
    }
    if esize < 128 || esize % 8 != 0 || esize > 4096 {
        put_st(val, esize)
        return W_ESIZE
    }
    if count > 1048576 / esize {
        put_st(val, count)
        return W_ASIZE
    }
    let bytes = count * esize
    let asec = (bytes + 511) / 512
    if elba < 2 || elba > last || asec > last - elba || (elba + asec > first && elba <= lastu) {
        put_st(val, elba)
        put_st(val + 1, asec)
        return W_ARRAY
    }
    arr_open(elba, bytes)
    c = 0xffffffff
    var o: u64 = 0
    while o < bytes {
        c = crc_byte(c, arr_byte(o))
        o += 1
    }
    if st(S_AERR) != 0 {
        return W_IO
    }
    c = c ^ 0xffffffff
    if c != acrc {
        put_st(val, acrc)
        put_st(val + 1, c)
        return W_ACRC
    }
    0
}

fn add_part(partno: u64, first: u64, n: u64, kind: u64) -> u64 {
    let d = st(S_DEVS)
    if d >= DEVMAX {
        put_st(S_PT_OVER, st(S_PT_OVER) + 1)
        return DEVS
    }
    var w: u64 = 0
    while w < DW {
        put_dw(d, w, 0)
        w += 1
    }
    put_dw(d, D_PRESENT, 1)
    put_dw(d, D_SECTORS, n)
    put_dw(d, D_FLAGS, dw(0, D_FLAGS))
    put_dw(d, D_LBS, dw(0, D_LBS))
    put_dw(d, D_DISK, 0)
    put_dw(d, D_START, first)
    put_dw(d, D_PARTNO, partno)
    put_dw(d, D_KIND, kind)
    if partno <= 15 {
        put_dw(d, D_MAJOR, 254)
        put_dw(d, D_MINOR, partno)
    } else {
        put_dw(d, D_MAJOR, 259)
        put_dw(d, D_MINOR, st(S_PT_EXTN))
        put_st(S_PT_EXTN, st(S_PT_EXTN) + 1)
    }
    put_st(S_DEVS, d + 1)
    d
}

fn refuse_entry(slot: u64, why: u64) {
    if st(S_PT_BADN) == 0 {
        put_st(S_PT_BADK, slot)
        put_st(S_PT_BADW, why)
    }
    put_st(S_PT_BADN, st(S_PT_BADN) + 1)
}

/// The UTF-16LE name of the entry at byte `o` of the array (72 bytes),
/// as UTF-8 into device `d`'s record.
fn keep_name(d: u64, o: u64) {
    let a = rec(d) + D_NAME * 8
    var n: u64 = 0
    var k: u64 = 0
    while k < 36 {
        var u = arr_byte(o + 2 * k) | (arr_byte(o + 2 * k + 1) << 8)
        if u == 0 {
            break
        }
        k += 1
        if u >= 0xd800 && u < 0xdc00 && k < 36 {
            let lo = arr_byte(o + 2 * k) | (arr_byte(o + 2 * k + 1) << 8)
            if lo >= 0xdc00 && lo < 0xe000 {
                u = 0x10000 + ((u - 0xd800) << 10) + (lo - 0xdc00)
                k += 1
            } else {
                u = 0xfffd
            }
        } else if u >= 0xd800 && u < 0xe000 {
            u = 0xfffd
        }
        if n + 4 > NAME_MAX {
            break
        }
        if u < 0x80 {
            put_b8(a + n, u)
            n += 1
        } else if u < 0x800 {
            put_b8(a + n, 0xc0 | (u >> 6))
            put_b8(a + n + 1, 0x80 | (u & 0x3f))
            n += 2
        } else if u < 0x10000 {
            put_b8(a + n, 0xe0 | (u >> 12))
            put_b8(a + n + 1, 0x80 | ((u >> 6) & 0x3f))
            put_b8(a + n + 2, 0x80 | (u & 0x3f))
            n += 3
        } else {
            put_b8(a + n, 0xf0 | (u >> 18))
            put_b8(a + n + 1, 0x80 | ((u >> 12) & 0x3f))
            put_b8(a + n + 2, 0x80 | ((u >> 6) & 0x3f))
            put_b8(a + n + 3, 0x80 | (u & 0x3f))
            n += 4
        }
    }
    put_dw(d, D_NAMELEN, n)
}

/// The partitions of the header at `h`'s entry array.
fn read_entries(h: u64) {
    let count = hx(h, HX_COUNT)
    let esize = hx(h, HX_ESIZE)
    let first = hx(h, HX_FIRST)
    let lastu = hx(h, HX_LAST)
    arr_open(hx(h, HX_ELBA), count * esize)
    var k: u64 = 0
    while k < count {
        let o = k * esize
        let t0 = arr_le64(o)
        let t1 = arr_le64(o + 8)
        if t0 != 0 || t1 != 0 {
            let f = arr_le64(o + 32)
            let l = arr_le64(o + 40)
            if f > l {
                refuse_entry(k + 1, E_ORDER)
            } else if f < first || l > lastu {
                refuse_entry(k + 1, E_USABLE)
            } else {
                let d = add_part(k + 1, f, l - f + 1, KIND_GPT)
                if d != DEVS {
                    put_dw(d, D_TYPE, t0)
                    put_dw(d, D_TYPE + 1, t1)
                    put_dw(d, D_UUID, arr_le64(o + 16))
                    put_dw(d, D_UUID + 1, arr_le64(o + 24))
                    put_dw(d, D_ATTR, arr_le64(o + 48))
                    keep_name(d, o + 56)
                }
            }
        }
        k += 1
    }
}

fn read_gpt() {
    let last = dw(0, D_SECTORS) - 1
    let pw = check_header(1, H_P, S_PT_VALP)
    let bw = check_header(last, H_B, S_PT_VALB)
    put_st(S_PT_WHYP, pw)
    put_st(S_PT_WHYB, bw)
    if pw != 0 && bw != 0 {
        put_st(S_PT_KIND, PT_GPTBAD)
        return
    }
    put_st(S_PT_KIND, PT_GPT)
    let h = if pw == 0 { H_P } else { H_B }
    put_st(S_PT_USED, if pw == 0 { 1 } else { 2 })
    if pw == 0 && hx(H_P, HX_ALT) != last {
        put_st(S_PT_ALT, hx(H_P, HX_ALT) + 1)
    }
    if pw == 0 && bw == 0 && (hx(H_P, HX_GUID) != hx(H_B, HX_GUID) || hx(H_P, HX_GUID + 1) != hx(H_B, HX_GUID + 1)
        || hx(H_P, HX_ACRC) != hx(H_B, HX_ACRC) || hx(H_P, HX_COUNT) != hx(H_B, HX_COUNT) || hx(H_P, HX_ESIZE) != hx(H_B, HX_ESIZE)
        || hx(H_P, HX_FIRST) != hx(H_B, HX_FIRST) || hx(H_P, HX_LAST) != hx(H_B, HX_LAST)) {
        put_st(S_PT_DIFF, 1)
    }
    read_entries(h)
}

fn read_mbr() {
    put_st(S_PT_KIND, PT_MBR)
    let p = scr()
    let cap = dw(0, D_SECTORS)
    put_st(S_PT_SIG, le32(p + 440))
    var k: u64 = 0
    while k < 4 {
        let r = p + 446 + 16 * k
        let t = b8(r + 4)
        let f = le32(r + 8)
        let n = le32(r + 12)
        if t != 0 && n != 0 {
            if t == 0x05 || t == 0x0f || t == 0x85 {
                if st(S_PT_EXT) == 0 {
                    put_st(S_PT_EXT, (k + 1) | (t << 8))
                }
            } else if f == 0 || f > cap || n > cap - f {
                refuse_entry(k + 1, E_DISK)
            } else {
                let d = add_part(k + 1, f, n, KIND_MBR)
                if d != DEVS {
                    put_dw(d, D_TYPE, t)
                    put_dw(d, D_UUID, st(S_PT_SIG))
                    put_dw(d, D_UUID + 1, k + 1)
                }
            }
        }
        k += 1
    }
}

/// Disk 0's partition table, read once at `start` (the header above);
/// what it found is said by `report`.
fn read_table() {
    put_st(S_PT_KIND, PT_NONE)
    put_st(S_PT_EXTN, 0)
    let lbs = dw(0, D_LBS)
    if lbs != 512 && lbs != 0 {
        put_st(S_PT_KIND, PT_LBS)
        return
    }
    if dw(0, D_SECTORS) < 3 {
        return
    }
    if read_scr(0, 1) != 0 {
        put_st(S_PT_KIND, PT_IO)
        return
    }
    let p = scr()
    if le16(p + 510) != 0xaa55 {
        return
    }
    var prot = false
    var flags_bad = false
    var k: u64 = 0
    while k < 4 {
        let r = p + 446 + 16 * k
        let boot = b8(r)
        if boot != 0 && boot != 0x80 {
            flags_bad = true
        }
        if b8(r + 4) == 0xee && le32(r + 8) == 1 {
            prot = true
        }
        k += 1
    }
    if prot {
        read_gpt()
    } else if flags_bad {
        put_st(S_PT_KIND, PT_NOTMBR)
    } else {
        read_mbr()
    }
}

// --- names: PARTUUID=, PARTLABEL= (kernel/fstab), /proc/partitions -------------

/// A hex digit's value, or 16.
fn hexval(c: u64) -> u64 {
    if c >= 48 && c <= 57 { c - 48 } else if c >= 97 && c <= 102 { c - 87 } else if c >= 65 && c <= 70 { c - 55 } else { 16 }
}

/// The `n` bytes at `a` as a GUID's or UUID's text (32 hex digits,
/// hyphens anywhere skipped, either case): (ok, the 16 bytes in the
/// text's order as two big-endian words).
pub fn uuid_text(a: u64, n: u64) -> (bool, u64, u64) {
    var hi: u64 = 0
    var lo: u64 = 0
    var got: u64 = 0
    var k: u64 = 0
    while k < n {
        let c = b8(a + k)
        if c != 45 {
            let v = hexval(c)
            if v == 16 || got == 32 {
                return (false, 0, 0)
            }
            if got < 16 {
                hi = (hi << 4) | v
            } else {
                lo = (lo << 4) | v
            }
            got += 1
        }
        k += 1
    }
    (got == 32, hi, lo)
}

/// A GPT GUID kept as two le64 words (its 16 bytes as on the disk), as
/// the text's two big-endian words (Appendix A: the first three fields
/// are little-endian on the disk).
fn guid_as_text(w0: u64, w1: u64) -> (u64, u64) {
    // Data1, Data2 and Data3 are w0's low 32, next 16 and top 16 bits
    // (little-endian numbers: their text is their value's hex); Data4's
    // eight bytes are w1's from its low byte up, in the text's order.
    var lo: u64 = 0
    var k: u64 = 0
    while k < 8 {
        lo = (lo << 8) | ((w1 >> (8 * k)) & 0xff)
        k += 1
    }
    (((w0 & 0xffffffff) << 32) | (((w0 >> 32) & 0xffff) << 16) | ((w0 >> 48) & 0xffff), lo)
}

/// Write a GUID (two le64 words as on the disk, or as Limine's
/// `struct limine_uuid` holds it) as its text.
pub fn put_guid(w0: u64, w1: u64) {
    let (hi, lo) = guid_as_text(w0, w1)
    log.hex_n(hi >> 32, 8)
    log.put("-")
    log.hex_n((hi >> 16) & 0xffff, 4)
    log.put("-")
    log.hex_n(hi & 0xffff, 4)
    log.put("-")
    log.hex_n(lo >> 48, 4)
    log.put("-")
    log.hex_n(lo & 0xffffffffffff, 12)
}

/// Write device `d`'s PARTUUID: a GPT partition's unique GUID, an MBR
/// partition's `ssssssss-nn` (as the guest's blkid prints them).
pub fn put_partuuid(d: u64) {
    if kind(d) == KIND_GPT {
        put_guid(dw(d, D_UUID), dw(d, D_UUID + 1))
    } else if kind(d) == KIND_MBR {
        log.hex_n(dw(d, D_UUID), 8)
        log.put("-")
        log.hex_n(dw(d, D_UUID + 1), 2)
    }
}

/// The partition whose PARTUUID is the `n` bytes at `a`, or DEVS.
pub fn by_partuuid(a: u64, n: u64) -> u64 {
    var d: u64 = 1
    if n == 11 && b8(a + 8) == 45 {
        // an MBR partition's: ssssssss-nn
        var sig: u64 = 0
        var k: u64 = 0
        while k < 8 {
            let v = hexval(b8(a + k))
            if v == 16 {
                return DEVS
            }
            sig = (sig << 4) | v
            k += 1
        }
        let p1 = hexval(b8(a + 9))
        let p2 = hexval(b8(a + 10))
        if p1 == 16 || p2 == 16 {
            return DEVS
        }
        while d < count() {
            if kind(d) == KIND_MBR && dw(d, D_UUID) == sig && dw(d, D_UUID + 1) == p1 * 16 + p2 {
                return d
            }
            d += 1
        }
        return DEVS
    }
    let (ok, hi, lo) = uuid_text(a, n)
    if !ok {
        return DEVS
    }
    while d < count() {
        if kind(d) == KIND_GPT {
            let (th, tl) = guid_as_text(dw(d, D_UUID), dw(d, D_UUID + 1))
            if th == hi && tl == lo {
                return d
            }
        }
        d += 1
    }
    DEVS
}

/// The GPT partition whose name (PARTLABEL) is the `n` bytes at `a`, or DEVS.
pub fn by_partlabel(a: u64, n: u64) -> u64 {
    var d: u64 = 1
    while d < count() {
        if kind(d) == KIND_GPT && dw(d, D_NAMELEN) == n {
            let p = rec(d) + D_NAME * 8
            var k: u64 = 0
            while k < n && b8(p + k) == b8(a + k) {
                k += 1
            }
            if k == n {
                return d
            }
        }
        d += 1
    }
    DEVS
}

// The renderer's sink (state words): every byte the text has is
// counted, and those in [off, off + n) are written at dst.
const S_SK_POS: u64 = 100
const S_SK_OFF: u64 = 101
const S_SK_N: u64 = 102
const S_SK_DST: u64 = 103

fn sk(c: u64) {
    let p = st(S_SK_POS)
    let off = st(S_SK_OFF)
    if p >= off && p - off < st(S_SK_N) {
        put_b8(st(S_SK_DST) + p - off, c)
    }
    put_st(S_SK_POS, p + 1)
}

fn sk_str(s: str) {
    for c in s.bytes() {
        sk(c as int as u64)
    }
}

/// `v` in decimal, right-aligned in `width` columns.
fn sk_num(v: u64, width: u64) {
    var pad = digits(v)
    while pad < width {
        sk(32)
        pad += 1
    }
    var p: u64 = 1
    while v / p >= 10 {
        p = p * 10
    }
    while p > 0 {
        sk(48 + v / p % 10)
        p = p / 10
    }
}

/// `/proc/partitions`' text, bytes [off, off + n) of it to the kernel
/// address `dst`: how many were written (n, or fewer at the end). Linux
/// 7.2.8's layout, measured on the guest (tests/mpx6-part.d/linux/): a
/// header line and an empty one, then a line a device, the disk first
/// and its partitions in their table's order: the major in 4 columns,
/// two blanks, the minor in 7, a blank, the size in KiB (sectors / 2,
/// rounded down) in 10, a blank, the name. The table does not change
/// after `start`, so a reader in pieces reads one text; it is made again
/// for each piece, so it may be longer than a page (128 partitions:
/// 4,152 bytes).
pub fn partitions_slice(dst: u64, off: u64, n: u64) -> u64 {
    if base() == 0 {
        // No disk: Linux's file then has the header alone.
        let h = "major minor  #blocks  name\n\n"
        var k: u64 = 0
        var w: u64 = 0
        for c in h.bytes() {
            if k >= off && w < n {
                put_b8(dst + w, c as int as u64)
                w += 1
            }
            k += 1
        }
        return w
    }
    put_st(S_SK_POS, 0)
    put_st(S_SK_OFF, off)
    put_st(S_SK_N, n)
    put_st(S_SK_DST, dst)
    sk_str("major minor  #blocks  name\n\n")
    var d: u64 = 0
    while d < count() {
        let (ma, mi) = numbers(d)
        sk_num(ma, 4)
        sk_str("  ")
        sk_num(mi, 7)
        sk(32)
        sk_num(dw(d, D_SECTORS) / 2, 10)
        sk(32)
        var i: u64 = 0
        while i < name_len(d) {
            sk(name_byte(d, i))
            i += 1
        }
        sk(10)
        d += 1
    }
    let total = st(S_SK_POS)
    if total <= off { 0 } else if total - off < n { total - off } else { n }
}

/// `/proc/partitions`' length in bytes.
pub fn partitions_len() -> u64 {
    if base() == 0 {
        return 28
    }
    let _w = partitions_slice(0, 0, 0)
    st(S_SK_POS)
}

// --- the buffer cache -------------------------------------------------------------

/// The write policy (R-px63-3): WRITE_THROUGH or WRITE_BACK.
pub fn policy() -> u64 {
    if base() == 0 { WRITE_THROUGH } else { st(S_POLICY) }
}

/// Set it (a mount's option, kernel/user's fstab): the old one. Going
/// to write-through writes what is dirty first.
pub fn set_policy(p: u64) -> u64 {
    if base() == 0 {
        return WRITE_THROUGH
    }
    let old = st(S_POLICY)
    if p == WRITE_THROUGH && old == WRITE_BACK {
        var d: u64 = 0
        while d < count() {
            let _s = sync(d)
            d += 1
        }
    }
    put_st(S_POLICY, p)
    old
}

fn hdr(s: u64) -> u64 {
    st(S_HDRS + s / HPF) + hh() + (s % HPF) * 32
}

fn hw(s: u64, w: u64) -> u64 {
    rd(hdr(s) + w * 8)
}

fn put_hw(s: u64, w: u64, v: u64) {
    wr(hdr(s) + w * 8, v)
}

fn shift_of(bsize: u64) -> u64 {
    if bsize == 1024 { 1 } else if bsize == 2048 { 2 } else if bsize == 4096 { 3 } else { 0 }
}

fn key(d: u64, bsize: u64, blk: u64) -> u64 {
    ((d + 1) << 56) | (shift_of(bsize) << 48) | (blk & 0xffffffffffff)
}

fn bucket(k: u64) -> u64 {
    // (masked to 32 bits first: wolf traps an overflow, and the hash
    // wants the low bits mixed, not the product's carries)
    let h = ((k ^ (k >> 9) ^ (k >> 56)) & 0xffffffff) * 0x9e3779b1
    st(S_HASH) + hh() + ((h >> 7) % BUCKETS) * 8
}

/// The slot holding key `k`, or NB.
fn find(k: u64) -> u64 {
    var s = rd(bucket(k))
    var guard: u64 = 0
    while s != 0 && guard <= NB {
        if hw(s - 1, H_KEY) == k {
            return s - 1
        }
        s = hw(s - 1, H_NEXT)
        guard += 1
    }
    NB
}

fn unhash(s: u64) {
    let k = hw(s, H_KEY)
    if k == 0 {
        return
    }
    let b = bucket(k)
    var p = rd(b)
    if p == s + 1 {
        wr(b, hw(s, H_NEXT))
    } else {
        var guard: u64 = 0
        while p != 0 && guard <= NB {
            let nx = hw(p - 1, H_NEXT)
            if nx == s + 1 {
                put_hw(p - 1, H_NEXT, hw(s, H_NEXT))
                p = 0
            } else {
                p = nx
            }
            guard += 1
        }
    }
    put_hw(s, H_KEY, 0)
    put_hw(s, H_NEXT, 0)
}

fn rehash(s: u64, k: u64) {
    let b = bucket(k)
    put_hw(s, H_KEY, k)
    put_hw(s, H_NEXT, rd(b))
    wr(b, s + 1)
}

/// The device, the block and the block size of slot `s`'s key.
fn parts(s: u64) -> (u64, u64, u64) {
    let k = hw(s, H_KEY)
    ((k >> 56) - 1, k & 0xffffffffffff, 512 << ((k >> 48) & 0xff))
}

/// Write slot `s` to its disk: 0 or -EIO.
fn write_slot(s: u64) -> u64 {
    let (d, blk, bsize) = parts(s)
    let per = bsize / 512
    let r = rw(d, vblk.T_OUT, blk * per, per, hw(s, H_PA))
    kbump(K_WRITES, 1)
    if hw(s, H_FLAGS) & B_DIRTY != 0 {
        // px68: a committed block written home has had its checkpoint
        put_hw(s, H_FLAGS, hw(s, H_FLAGS) & !(B_DIRTY | B_JMETA))
        put_st(S_COUNT + K_DIRTY, st(S_COUNT + K_DIRTY) - 1)
    }
    r
}

/// A slot for a new key: one never made, else the clock hand's next
/// not looked at since it last passed, written first when dirty. NB when
/// no frame can be had for a new one and every made one failed to write.
fn victim() -> u64 {
    if st(S_MADE) < NB {
        let pa = frames.alloc() else 0
        if pa != 0 {
            let s = st(S_MADE)
            put_st(S_MADE, s + 1)
            put_hw(s, H_PA, pa)
            put_hw(s, H_FLAGS, 0)
            put_hw(s, H_KEY, 0)
            put_hw(s, H_NEXT, 0)
            return s
        }
    }
    let made = st(S_MADE)
    if made == 0 {
        return NB
    }
    var n: u64 = 0
    while n < 3 * made {
        let s = st(S_HAND) % made
        put_st(S_HAND, s + 1)
        let f = hw(s, H_FLAGS)
        if f & B_JRUN != 0 {
            n += 1 // px68: the running transaction's: not written home, not taken
            continue
        }
        if f & B_REF != 0 {
            put_hw(s, H_FLAGS, f & !B_REF)
        } else {
            if f & B_DIRTY != 0 {
                let w = write_slot(s)
                if w != 0 {
                    n += 1
                    continue
                }
            }
            unhash(s)
            put_hw(s, H_FLAGS, 0)
            kbump(K_EVICTS, 1)
            return s
        }
        n += 1
    }
    put_st(S_PINNED, st(S_PINNED) + 1)
    if !said(0, 8) {
        log.put("block: no buffer to be had: every one of the ")
        log.dec(made as int)
        log.put(" is held by a running transaction or would not write")
        log.end()
    }
    NB
}

/// Block `blk` (of `bsize` bytes) of disk `d`, read when the cache does
/// not hold it: its address (through the HHDM), good until the next
/// `get` or `fresh`; 0 on an I/O error, past the disk, or with no
/// buffer to be had.
pub fn get(d: u64, blk: u64, bsize: u64) -> u64 {
    if d >= count() || shift_of(bsize) == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let k = key(d, bsize, blk)
    let s = find(k)
    if s != NB {
        kbump(K_HITS, 1)
        put_hw(s, H_FLAGS, hw(s, H_FLAGS) | B_REF)
        return hw(s, H_PA) + hh()
    }
    let v = victim()
    if v == NB {
        return 0
    }
    let per = bsize / 512
    let r = rw(d, vblk.T_IN, blk * per, per, hw(v, H_PA))
    kbump(K_MISSES, 1)
    if r != 0 {
        return 0
    }
    rehash(v, k)
    put_hw(v, H_FLAGS, B_VALID | B_REF)
    hw(v, H_PA) + hh()
}

/// Block `blk` of disk `d` as a new block: its buffer zeroed, not read
/// (a block just allocated, all of which the caller will write): its
/// address, as `get`'s; 0 with no buffer.
pub fn fresh(d: u64, blk: u64, bsize: u64) -> u64 {
    if d >= count() || shift_of(bsize) == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let k = key(d, bsize, blk)
    var s = find(k)
    if s == NB {
        s = victim()
        if s == NB {
            return 0
        }
        rehash(s, k)
    }
    let va = hw(s, H_PA) + hh()
    // # Safety: the buffer's frame, through the HHDM.
    unsafe { pax_tree_zero(va, bsize) }
    // px68: a block in the running transaction stays in it (a new
    // indirect block made again); a committed one's old contents are no
    // longer what it holds, so it is no longer awaiting a checkpoint.
    put_hw(s, H_FLAGS, (hw(s, H_FLAGS) & (B_DIRTY | B_JRUN)) | B_VALID | B_REF)
    kbump(K_FRESH, 1)
    va
}

/// Block `blk` of disk `d` has been changed in its buffer: written now
/// (write-through) or marked (write-back). 0, -EIO, or -ENOSPC when the
/// block is not in the cache (a caller that broke the rule above).
pub fn dirty(d: u64, blk: u64, bsize: u64) -> u64 {
    let s = find(key(d, bsize, blk))
    if s == NB {
        return neg(ENOSPC)
    }
    if st(S_POLICY) == WRITE_THROUGH && hw(s, H_FLAGS) & B_JRUN == 0 {
        return write_slot(s)
    }
    let f = hw(s, H_FLAGS)
    if f & B_DIRTY != 0 {
        kbump(K_REDIRTY, 1)
        return 0
    }
    put_hw(s, H_FLAGS, f | B_DIRTY)
    put_st(S_COUNT + K_DIRTY, st(S_COUNT + K_DIRTY) + 1)
    0
}

/// Write every dirty buffer of disk `d`, then FLUSH it: 0 or -EIO (the
/// first error; the rest are still tried).
pub fn sync(d: u64) -> u64 {
    if d >= count() {
        return 0
    }
    var err: u64 = 0
    var s: u64 = 0
    while s < st(S_MADE) {
        // px68: a running transaction's buffer is the journal's to write
        if hw(s, H_FLAGS) & (B_DIRTY | B_JRUN) == B_DIRTY && hw(s, H_KEY) >> 56 == d + 1 {
            let w = write_slot(s)
            if w != 0 && err == 0 {
                err = w
            }
        }
        s += 1
    }
    let f = flush(d)
    if err != 0 { err } else { f }
}

// --- px68: the journal's side of the cache ----------------------------------------

/// Block `blk` of disk `d`, in the cache (the caller changed it just
/// now), joins the running transaction: marked dirty, B_JRUN and B_JMETA,
/// never written through. 1 when it was not in the transaction before,
/// 0 when it was, -ENOSPC when it is not in the cache.
pub fn jdirty(d: u64, blk: u64, bsize: u64) -> u64 {
    let s = find(key(d, bsize, blk))
    if s == NB {
        return neg(ENOSPC)
    }
    let f = hw(s, H_FLAGS)
    if f & B_DIRTY == 0 {
        put_st(S_COUNT + K_DIRTY, st(S_COUNT + K_DIRTY) + 1)
    } else {
        kbump(K_REDIRTY, 1)
    }
    put_hw(s, H_FLAGS, f | B_DIRTY | B_JRUN | B_JMETA)
    if f & B_JRUN == 0 { 1 } else { 0 }
}

/// Block `blk` of disk `d`, freed in the running transaction, leaves it:
/// whether it was in it. Its buffer is no longer dirty (what it holds now
/// was never committed, and the block is free: the disk keeps what the
/// last commit left there).
pub fn jforget(d: u64, blk: u64, bsize: u64) -> bool {
    let s = find(key(d, bsize, blk))
    if s == NB {
        return false
    }
    let f = hw(s, H_FLAGS)
    if f & B_JRUN == 0 {
        return false
    }
    if f & B_DIRTY != 0 {
        put_st(S_COUNT + K_DIRTY, st(S_COUNT + K_DIRTY) - 1)
    }
    put_hw(s, H_FLAGS, f & !(B_DIRTY | B_JRUN | B_JMETA))
    true
}

/// The physical address of block `blk`'s buffer (the journal writes it
/// to the log from there), 0 when the cache does not hold it.
pub fn buffer_pa(d: u64, blk: u64, bsize: u64) -> u64 {
    let s = find(key(d, bsize, blk))
    if s == NB { 0 } else { hw(s, H_PA) }
}

/// Block `blk`'s commit block is on the disk: its buffer leaves the
/// running transaction, a committed block awaiting its checkpoint.
pub fn jdone(d: u64, blk: u64, bsize: u64) {
    let s = find(key(d, bsize, blk))
    if s != NB {
        put_hw(s, H_FLAGS, (hw(s, H_FLAGS) & !B_JRUN) | B_JMETA)
    }
}

/// data=ordered (px68): every dirty buffer of disk `d` that is neither in
/// a running transaction nor journalled metadata (a file's data) written
/// home; no FLUSH (the journal's own comes after its log blocks). 0 or
/// the first -EIO.
pub fn write_data(d: u64) -> u64 {
    var err: u64 = 0
    var s: u64 = 0
    while s < st(S_MADE) {
        if hw(s, H_FLAGS) & (B_DIRTY | B_JRUN | B_JMETA) == B_DIRTY && hw(s, H_KEY) >> 56 == d + 1 {
            let w = write_slot(s)
            if w != 0 && err == 0 {
                err = w
            }
        }
        s += 1
    }
    err
}

/// The checkpoint (px68): every committed block of disk `d` awaiting it
/// written home (none of a running transaction's: the journal asks only
/// when none is open); no FLUSH. 0 or the first -EIO.
pub fn checkpoint(d: u64) -> u64 {
    var err: u64 = 0
    var s: u64 = 0
    while s < st(S_MADE) {
        let f = hw(s, H_FLAGS)
        if f & (B_DIRTY | B_JRUN | B_JMETA) == (B_DIRTY | B_JMETA) && hw(s, H_KEY) >> 56 == d + 1 {
            let w = write_slot(s)
            if w != 0 && err == 0 {
                err = w
            }
        } else if f & (B_DIRTY | B_JMETA) == B_JMETA && hw(s, H_KEY) >> 56 == d + 1 {
            put_hw(s, H_FLAGS, f & !B_JMETA)
        }
        s += 1
    }
    err
}

/// Forget every buffer of disk `d` (after `sync`, at an unmount): a
/// later `get` reads the disk again.
pub fn forget(d: u64) {
    var s: u64 = 0
    while s < st(S_MADE) {
        if hw(s, H_KEY) >> 56 == d + 1 && hw(s, H_FLAGS) & B_DIRTY == 0 {
            unhash(s)
            put_hw(s, H_FLAGS, 0)
        }
        s += 1
    }
}

/// px68: misses that found no buffer to take (every one held by a
/// running transaction, or failing to write).
pub fn pinned() -> u64 {
    if base() == 0 { 0 } else { st(S_PINNED) }
}

/// Buffers made (frames held by the cache).
pub fn buffers() -> u64 {
    if base() == 0 { 0 } else { st(S_MADE) }
}

/// The disk's lines (kernel/vblk's, then the layer's), then what its
/// partition table holds (px66):
///
///     block: vda: <s> sectors (<b> bytes), 254:0, <rw|read-only>[, flush]
///     block: vda: GPT, disk <guid>, <n> entries of <e> bytes at LBA <l>, usable LBAs <f>-<u>; <p> partitions
///     block: vda2: 254:2, sectors <f>-<l> (<n> sectors, <b> bytes), type <guid>, PARTUUID <guid>, name "<name>"
///     block: vda: MBR, signature <ssssssss>; <p> partitions
///     block: vda1: 254:1, sectors <f>-<l> (<n> sectors, <b> bytes), type 0x<tt>, PARTUUID <ssssssss>-<nn>
///
/// with a line for each refusal and for each header not used (the
/// module's header says which). A disk with no table (no 0x55aa) has the
/// first line alone.
pub fn report() {
    if count() == 0 {
        return
    }
    vblk.report()
    log.put("block: vda: ")
    log.dec(dw(0, D_SECTORS) as int)
    log.put(" sectors (")
    log.dec((dw(0, D_SECTORS) * 512) as int)
    log.put(" bytes), 254:0, ")
    log.put(if dw(0, D_FLAGS) & F_RO != 0 { "read-only" } else { "read-write" })
    if dw(0, D_FLAGS) & F_FLUSH != 0 {
        log.put(", flush")
    }
    log.end()
    table_report()
}

fn put_why(w: u64, val: u64) {
    if w == W_IO {
        log.put("could not be read")
    } else if w == W_SIG {
        log.put("has no signature (EFI PART)")
    } else if w == W_REV {
        log.put("has revision ")
        log.hex_n(st(val), 8)
        log.put(" (1.0 is read)")
    } else if w == W_HSIZE {
        log.put("has a header size of ")
        log.dec(st(val) as int)
        log.put(" (92 to 512)")
    } else if w == W_HCRC {
        log.put("fails its CRC32 (")
        log.hex_n(st(val), 8)
        log.put(" stored, ")
        log.hex_n(st(val + 1), 8)
        log.put(" computed)")
    } else if w == W_MYLBA {
        log.put("says it is at LBA ")
        log.dec(st(val) as int)
    } else if w == W_USABLE {
        log.put("has usable LBAs ")
        log.dec(st(val) as int)
        log.put("-")
        log.dec(st(val + 1) as int)
        log.put(", not inside the disk")
    } else if w == W_ESIZE {
        log.put("has entries of ")
        log.dec(st(val) as int)
        log.put(" bytes (128 or a larger multiple of 8, at most 4096, are read)")
    } else if w == W_ASIZE {
        log.put("has ")
        log.dec(st(val) as int)
        log.put(" entries (an array of at most 1 MiB is read)")
    } else if w == W_ARRAY {
        log.put("has its entry array at LBA ")
        log.dec(st(val) as int)
        log.put(" (")
        log.dec(st(val + 1) as int)
        log.put(" sectors), outside the space a table may use")
    } else {
        log.put("fails its entry array's CRC32 (")
        log.hex_n(st(val), 8)
        log.put(" stored, ")
        log.hex_n(st(val + 1), 8)
        log.put(" computed)")
    }
}

fn table_report() {
    let k = st(S_PT_KIND)
    let last = dw(0, D_SECTORS) - 1
    if k == PT_NONE || k == PT_NOTMBR {
        return
    }
    if k == PT_IO {
        log.line("block: vda: the partition table: LBA 0 could not be read; vda is the whole disk")
        return
    }
    if k == PT_LBS {
        log.put("block: vda: refused: a partition table on logical blocks of ")
        log.dec(dw(0, D_LBS) as int)
        log.put(" bytes (PAX reads tables of 512-byte blocks); not read, vda is the whole disk")
        log.end()
        return
    }
    if k == PT_GPTBAD {
        log.put("block: vda: refused: no valid GPT: the primary header at LBA 1 ")
        put_why(st(S_PT_WHYP), S_PT_VALP)
        log.put(", the backup at LBA ")
        log.dec(last as int)
        log.put(" ")
        put_why(st(S_PT_WHYB), S_PT_VALB)
        log.put("; no partitions, vda is the whole disk")
        log.end()
        return
    }
    if k == PT_GPT {
        let h = if st(S_PT_USED) == 1 { H_P } else { H_B }
        if st(S_PT_USED) == 2 {
            log.put("block: vda: the primary GPT header at LBA 1 ")
            put_why(st(S_PT_WHYP), S_PT_VALP)
            log.put("; the backup at LBA ")
            log.dec(last as int)
            log.put(" is used")
            log.end()
        } else if st(S_PT_WHYB) != 0 {
            log.put("block: vda: the backup GPT header at LBA ")
            log.dec(last as int)
            log.put(" ")
            put_why(st(S_PT_WHYB), S_PT_VALB)
            log.put("; the primary is used")
            log.end()
        }
        if st(S_PT_ALT) != 0 {
            log.put("block: vda: the primary GPT header's alternate LBA is ")
            log.dec((st(S_PT_ALT) - 1) as int)
            log.put(", not the disk's last, ")
            log.dec(last as int)
            log.put("; the backup was read at the last")
            log.end()
        }
        if st(S_PT_DIFF) != 0 {
            log.line("block: vda: the backup GPT header disagrees with the primary; the primary is used")
        }
        log.put("block: vda: GPT, disk ")
        put_guid(hx(h, HX_GUID), hx(h, HX_GUID + 1))
        log.put(", ")
        log.dec(hx(h, HX_COUNT) as int)
        log.put(" entries of ")
        log.dec(hx(h, HX_ESIZE) as int)
        log.put(" bytes at LBA ")
        log.dec(hx(h, HX_ELBA) as int)
        log.put(", usable LBAs ")
        log.dec(hx(h, HX_FIRST) as int)
        log.put("-")
        log.dec(hx(h, HX_LAST) as int)
    } else {
        log.put("block: vda: MBR, signature ")
        log.hex_n(st(S_PT_SIG), 8)
    }
    log.put("; ")
    log.dec((count() - 1) as int)
    log.put(if count() == 2 { " partition" } else { " partitions" })
    log.end()
    if st(S_PT_EXT) != 0 {
        log.put("block: vda: refused: an extended partition (type 0x")
        log.hex_n(st(S_PT_EXT) >> 8, 2)
        log.put(") in slot ")
        log.dec((st(S_PT_EXT) & 0xff) as int)
        log.put(": its logical partitions are not read")
        log.end()
    }
    if st(S_PT_BADN) != 0 {
        log.put("block: vda: refused: ")
        log.dec(st(S_PT_BADN) as int)
        log.put(if st(S_PT_BADN) == 1 { " entry" } else { " entries" })
        log.put(", the first slot ")
        log.dec(st(S_PT_BADK) as int)
        let w = st(S_PT_BADW)
        log.put(if w == E_ORDER { ": its first LBA is after its last" } else if w == E_USABLE { ": outside the usable LBAs" } else { ": past the disk's end, or at LBA 0" })
        log.end()
    }
    if st(S_PT_OVER) != 0 {
        log.put("block: vda: refused: ")
        log.dec(st(S_PT_OVER) as int)
        log.put(" partitions past the most PAX keeps (")
        log.dec((DEVMAX - 1) as int)
        log.put(")")
        log.end()
    }
    var d: u64 = 1
    while d < count() {
        log.put("block: ")
        put_name(d)
        log.put(": ")
        log.dec(dw(d, D_MAJOR) as int)
        log.put(":")
        log.dec(dw(d, D_MINOR) as int)
        log.put(", sectors ")
        log.dec(dw(d, D_START) as int)
        log.put("-")
        log.dec((dw(d, D_START) + dw(d, D_SECTORS) - 1) as int)
        log.put(" (")
        log.dec(dw(d, D_SECTORS) as int)
        log.put(" sectors, ")
        log.dec((dw(d, D_SECTORS) * 512) as int)
        log.put(" bytes), type ")
        if dw(d, D_KIND) == KIND_GPT {
            put_guid(dw(d, D_TYPE), dw(d, D_TYPE + 1))
        } else {
            log.put("0x")
            log.hex_n(dw(d, D_TYPE), 2)
        }
        log.put(", PARTUUID ")
        put_partuuid(d)
        if dw(d, D_KIND) == KIND_GPT {
            log.put(", name ")
            log.put_byte(34)
            let p = rec(d) + D_NAME * 8
            var i: u64 = 0
            while i < dw(d, D_NAMELEN) {
                log.put_byte(b8(p + i) as int)
                i += 1
            }
            log.put_byte(34)
        }
        log.end()
        d += 1
    }
}

/// The books' line, and one for each partition a request was made to:
///
///     block: vda: <r> reads (<si> sectors), <w> writes (<so> sectors), <f> flushes, <e> errors, <x> refused; cache <m> buffers, <h> hits, <mi> misses, <ev> taken back, <fr> fresh, <rd> written again while dirty, <dn> dirty, policy write-through|write-back
///     block: vda2: <r> reads (<si> sectors), <w> writes (<so> sectors), <e> errors, <x> refused
///
/// The disk's counts are every request its driver was given, its
/// partitions' included.
pub fn summary() {
    if count() == 0 {
        return
    }
    log.put("block: vda: ")
    log.dec(dw(0, D_READS) as int)
    log.put(" reads (")
    log.dec(dw(0, D_SIN) as int)
    log.put(" sectors), ")
    log.dec(dw(0, D_WRITES) as int)
    log.put(" writes (")
    log.dec(dw(0, D_SOUT) as int)
    log.put(" sectors), ")
    log.dec(dw(0, D_FLUSHES) as int)
    log.put(" flushes, ")
    log.dec(dw(0, D_ERRORS) as int)
    log.put(" errors, ")
    log.dec(dw(0, D_REFUSED) as int)
    log.put(" refused; cache ")
    log.dec(st(S_MADE) as int)
    log.put(" buffers, ")
    log.dec(st(S_COUNT + K_HITS) as int)
    log.put(" hits, ")
    log.dec(st(S_COUNT + K_MISSES) as int)
    log.put(" misses, ")
    log.dec(st(S_COUNT + K_EVICTS) as int)
    log.put(" taken back, ")
    log.dec(st(S_COUNT + K_FRESH) as int)
    log.put(" fresh, ")
    log.dec(st(S_COUNT + K_REDIRTY) as int)
    log.put(" written again while dirty, ")
    log.dec(st(S_COUNT + K_DIRTY) as int)
    log.put(" dirty, policy ")
    log.put(if st(S_POLICY) == WRITE_BACK { "write-back" } else { "write-through" })
    log.end()
    var d: u64 = 1
    while d < count() {
        if dw(d, D_READS) + dw(d, D_WRITES) + dw(d, D_REFUSED) != 0 {
            log.put("block: ")
            put_name(d)
            log.put(": ")
            log.dec(dw(d, D_READS) as int)
            log.put(" reads (")
            log.dec(dw(d, D_SIN) as int)
            log.put(" sectors), ")
            log.dec(dw(d, D_WRITES) as int)
            log.put(" writes (")
            log.dec(dw(d, D_SOUT) as int)
            log.put(" sectors), ")
            log.dec(dw(d, D_ERRORS) as int)
            log.put(" errors, ")
            log.dec(dw(d, D_REFUSED) as int)
            log.put(" refused")
            log.end()
        }
        d += 1
    }
}

/// Copy `n` bytes between kernel addresses (a buffer and a caller's
/// frame): kernel/ext2's and kmain_blk's.
pub fn copy_bytes(dst: u64, src: u64, n: u64) {
    // # Safety: both ranges inside frames the callers hold, through the
    // HHDM; they do not overlap.
    unsafe { pax_tree_copy(dst, src, n) }
}
